WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:06.750
From hidden Hamilton references to secret
cameos and West Side Story easter eggs, Lin-Manuel

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:10.760
Miranda's In The Heights is filled with sizzling
details that will make you love it even more.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:15.650
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm revealing over 50 amazing things

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:18.460
you missed and fun facts about the musical.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:22.780
When Kevin Rosario rings Stanford about his
daughter's tuition fees, the cheery music

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:27.630
he hears when he's on hold is a muzak version
of "You'll Be Back", King's George III's break-up

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:29.990
song to the American colonists in Hamilton.

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:31.740
"Hi Mr Rosario?"

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.089
"Yes, yes, I'm here."

00:00:34.089 --> 00:00:37.760
The tune is more than just a fun easter egg
for Hamilfans though because it actually fits

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:42.159
the moment perfectly as Kevin is trying to
get his daughter back to university after

00:00:42.159 --> 00:00:46.710
she's decided to drop out and the song foreshadows
the fact that eventually Nina chooses to go

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.399
back to Stanford.

00:00:48.399 --> 00:00:52.890
Lin-Manuel Miranda makes a fun appearance
as the piragüero who has an intense rivalry

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:57.550
with an ice cream seller, which is where the
next Hamilton connection comes in as the Mister

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:01.539
Softee truck driver is played by Christopher
Jackson, who starred as George Washington

00:01:01.539 --> 00:01:03.629
in Miranda's revolutionary musical.

00:01:03.629 --> 00:01:08.509
And before that, Jackson also starred in the
stage version of In The Heights, originating

00:01:08.509 --> 00:01:11.729
the role of Benny, who's played by Corey Hawkins
in the film.

00:01:11.729 --> 00:01:16.939
Casting Jackson and Miranda as rival street
vendors in the adaptation is an amusing in-joke

00:01:16.939 --> 00:01:21.170
about their real-life close friendship and
their many creative collaborations,

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:25.950
from the Heights and Hamilton musicals, to
the improv hip-hop group Freestyle Love Supreme,

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:30.450
and the Disney musical Moana where Jackson
provided the singing voice for her father.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:35.259
By the way, make sure you stick around till
the very end of the movie's credits as there's

00:01:35.259 --> 00:01:39.620
an extra, fun little scene where the piragüero
gets his own back on the rival who outsold

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:43.540
him earlier in the film when Mister Softee's
truck breaks down.

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:48.229
Cue the piragua guy taking full advantage
of the heat and indulging in a bit of surge

00:01:48.229 --> 00:01:50.970
pricing now his competition's out of the game.

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:56.310
The spectacular 96,000 song is one of the
highlights of the movie, and as director Jon

00:01:56.310 --> 00:02:01.280
M Chu says, it isn't just "an 'I Want' song
for one character, but for the whole neighbourhood".

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.579
The song's number has several layers of significance
in the story.

00:02:04.579 --> 00:02:08.860
First of all, Miranda says he chose 96,000
as the prize amount because although it'd

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:13.400
be enough to make a person's life a little
easier, it wouldn't be enough to completely

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:14.900
change things.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:19.120
Miranda also says that for him personally
"the number '96' has a subconscious wealth

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.820
line connotation" because, as a child growing
up in New York City, he noticed the difference

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:28.540
between "the price of living on 94th Street
where he went to school, versus 200th Street,

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:29.709
where he lived".

00:02:29.709 --> 00:02:34.379
Which resulted in him viewing 96th Street,
the northern border of the Upper West and

00:02:34.379 --> 00:02:39.060
East Sides, as a kind of "economic dividing
line", something which is also referenced

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.299
in the opening song.

00:02:40.299 --> 00:02:43.069
"I've never been north of 96th Street."

00:02:43.069 --> 00:02:47.830
And there may even be a reference to 96,000
in Hamilton which was Miranda's next Broadway

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:49.700
show after In The Heights.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:55.489
In the song "Right Hand Man", the line "32,000
troops in New York Harbor" repeats three times,

00:02:55.489 --> 00:02:58.650
and 32,000 times three equals 96,000.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:04.440
A detail that only those familiar with the
stage version of 96,000 may have noticed is

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:08.849
the lyric change when Benny fantasizes about
what he'd do if he's the lotto winner.

00:03:08.849 --> 00:03:13.590
In the movie, Benny says he'll play golf with
Tiger Woods, whereas in the original song,

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:17.310
Benny brags he'd be so rich that he'd have
Donald Trump as his caddy.

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:21.570
In an interview with Variety, Miranda explained
that when he originally wrote In The Heights,

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:27.200
he viewed Trump as "an avatar for the Monopoly
Man, just a famous rich person", but now "time

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:30.060
made a fool of that lyric, so they changed
it."

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:34.280
The movie's version of the song is an incredible
dance extravaganza that was filmed at New

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.030
York's Highbridge Pool.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:40.159
And shooting the sequence was an insanely
difficult feat to pull off, with the cast

00:03:40.159 --> 00:03:45.349
and dancers having to perform in freezing
cold water, while thunderstorms kept interrupting

00:03:45.349 --> 00:03:46.349
filming.

00:03:46.349 --> 00:03:50.709
In fact, the weather was so bad that the sky
and pool had to be colour-corrected in post-production

00:03:50.709 --> 00:03:54.780
as, in the film, it's supposed to be one of
the hottest days of the year!

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:59.420
The aerial shots of Vanessa with an ensemble
of synchronised swimmers moving in geometric

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:04.440
patterns channel Busby Berkeley's renowned
choreography from classic musicals like Million

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:05.440
Dollar Mermaid.

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:10.709
However, achieving the perfect overhead shots
proved a lot harder than initially anticipated.

00:04:10.709 --> 00:04:15.780
Firstly, drone filming is prohibited in New
York and a crane had to be carefully constructed

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:18.720
to avoid damaging tunnels below the pool.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:22.320
Then whenever the swimmers around Vanessa
moved, the ripples and splashes caused the

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:24.630
tube she floated on to drift off.

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:29.639
In the end, they decided to film Vanessa separately
and digitally splice her footage in with the

00:04:29.639 --> 00:04:30.970
other swimmers.

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:35.950
Even then, Vanessa's pool ring kept moving
around during filming until Jon Chu decided

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:39.889
to wade into the water himself and hold it
steady for the shot.

00:04:39.889 --> 00:04:43.750
Another interesting detail is that although
the movie uses lots of bright colour to fit

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:48.600
the story's celebratory feel, for this scene
Vanessa was dressed in a black bikini to help

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.690
her stand out in a sea of colour.

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:55.830
There are shades of West Side Story in When
The Sun Goes Down, Benny and Nina's love song

00:04:55.830 --> 00:05:00.260
about their soon-to-be long-distance relationship,
which they sing on a fire escape,

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:04.870
reminiscent of Tony and Maria's love duet
Tonight, whose own roots are in the iconic

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:08.199
"balcony scene" from Shakespeare's Romeo And
Juliet.

00:05:08.199 --> 00:05:12.090
Interestingly, some of Miranda's own favourite
childhood memories are "walking through his

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:16.870
neighbourhood and looking up at the fire escapes"
And he got his own West Side fire escape moment

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:19.430
in the new movie during the Carnaval song.

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:23.540
And according to Jon Chu, filming that scene
led to an especially touching moment when

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:27.960
the whole cast chanted Miranda's name in gratitude
at what he'd created.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:33.300
Funnily enough, during location shoots for
In The Heights in 2019, Steven Spielberg was

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:38.700
mere blocks away filming his new take on West
Side Story, and Miranda even snuck over to

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:39.700
say hello!

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:43.810
And actually, Miranda has some history with
West Side Story himself, having directed the

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:47.790
musical at high school, translated some of
Stephen Sondheim's lyrics into Spanish for

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:53.580
the show's Broadway revival in 2009, and Sondheim's
influence is also found in both In The Heights

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:55.690
as well as Hamilton.

00:05:55.690 --> 00:06:00.660
Miranda played the lead part of Usnavi when
the show opened Off-Broadway in 2007.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:05.550
And originally, he was going to reprise that
role for the musical's big-screen adaptation

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:10.360
when the movie rights were picked up by Universal
after the show won Best Musical, among other

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:12.509
awards, at the Tonys in 2008.

00:06:12.509 --> 00:06:18.110
But after the screen version fell into development
hell, bouncing around various backers before

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:22.280
Warner Brothers won the rights in a bidding
war ten years later, Miranda felt he was too

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:23.890
old to play the part anymore.

00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:28.330
In the opening song, as Usnavi sings about
emigrating from the Dominican Republic, he

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:31.940
looks wistfully at the map of the Caribbean
on the wall of his bodega.

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:36.560
To make this map unique and give it some character,
the movie's production designer Nelson Coates

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:39.340
decorated it with items Usnavi sells in the
store.

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:43.470
There are Pepperidge Farm Goldfish crackers
to represent the fish in the sea, as well

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:48.590
as lids from cans, perhaps a reference to
the tin of condensed milk Abuela Claudia recommends

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:50.530
he use as a milk substitute.

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:54.889
The Dominican Republic itself is made up of
various pieces of coloured tiles in a form

00:06:54.889 --> 00:06:59.500
that resembles the country's flag, which ties
in nicely with the opening song's chorus line,

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:02.009
"In the Heights I hang my flag up on display."

00:07:02.009 --> 00:07:07.120
And there was a cute moment you might have
missed when Vanessa first walks into the store

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:12.010
and Benny and Sonny are trying to get Usnavi
to work up the courage to ask her out.

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:15.510
Notice the little rose that Benny gives to
Sonny which he later places on the coffee

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:17.850
cup Usnavi is about to give her.

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:21.009
Usnavi isn't having any of it though and tosses
the flower away.

00:07:21.009 --> 00:07:26.050
Speaking to The Hollywood Reporter, Miranda
said the movie's production designer recreated

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:30.480
an In The Heights doodle Miranda did when
he was in college and placed it on the piragüero's

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:31.480
cart.

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:35.600
It's only on screen for a second during the
main movie but I managed to spot it right

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:39.970
here in this blink-and-you'll-miss-it moment,
and it gets a smidge more screen time in the

00:07:39.970 --> 00:07:41.129
post-credits scene.

00:07:41.129 --> 00:07:45.810
In a change from the Broadway production,
Nina's mother doesn't appear in the movie;

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:50.190
however, there's a little easter egg to actress
Priscilla Lopez who played her on stage via

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:54.800
the photo of her next to the TV during Nina's
childhood flashback.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:59.000
Another nice detail from that scene of Nina
as a child is the rainbow on the top she's

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:01.790
wearing when her father gives her a new computer.

00:08:01.790 --> 00:08:06.430
As a symbol of Nina's restored hope for the
future, she also wears a top with a similar

00:08:06.430 --> 00:08:10.500
rainbow towards the end of the movie when
she goes to the protest with Sonny and then

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:14.650
explains to her father how she can use her
education at Stanford to help her community

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:17.180
and those who are undocumented.

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:21.270
By the way, Jimmy Smits, who plays Nina's
father in the film, has a previous connection

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:22.270
with In The Heights.

00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:26.449
When he first saw the musical Off-Broadway,
he was so blown away that he told Miranda

00:08:26.449 --> 00:08:28.850
that "if they needed anything, he was there
for them."

00:08:28.850 --> 00:08:34.290
And that offer led to Smits voicing the national
commercial for the musical's move to Broadway.

00:08:34.290 --> 00:08:38.550
During Nina's song "Breathe", as she's welcomed
back by the community, one of the original

00:08:38.550 --> 00:08:42.760
Broadway ensemble cast members Doreen Montalvo
has a cameo.

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:47.840
She sadly passed away in 2020 and there's
a tribute to her with the mural "Doreen Para

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:49.700
Siempre" in the movie's credits.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:53.010
Montalvo was the first actress to audition
for the musical.

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:56.950
She's also the voice of the bolero singer
on the scratched record that plays as Usnavi

00:08:56.950 --> 00:09:00.930
is planning to leave New York and return to
DR.

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:04.620
Miranda has said that he played the piragüero
as a "love letter to his grandfather".

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:09.180
So, those glasses you see around the piragua
guy's neck actually belonged to Miranda's

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:13.840
abuelo, and he also carried his grandad's
"cowboy novels" with him during filming, and

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:17.310
I noticed him reading one of those books at
the start of his song.

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:22.350
Speaking of grandparents, the character Abuela
Claudia was based on Miranda's real-life grandmother,

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:26.810
and she's played by the same actress who originated
the role on stage, Olga Merediz.

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:31.580
Seth Stewart, who was part of the ensemble
in Hamilton and played Graffiti Pete during

00:09:31.580 --> 00:09:36.570
the Off-Broadway and Broadway runs of In The
Heights, pops up in a cameo in the club as

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:37.570
a bartender.

00:09:37.570 --> 00:09:41.061
And, of course, we can't forget the movie's
lead Anthony Ramos, who before now was best

00:09:41.061 --> 00:09:45.060
known for his double roles of John Laurens
and Hamilton's son.

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:48.610
Miranda's mum and dad also get a cameo during
the song Breathe.

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:53.980
As Nina walks through her block, Luis Miranda
and Dr Luz Towns-Miranda pop up to greet her

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:54.980
return.

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:55.980
"Welcome home."

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:59.890
It feels appropriate that his parents should
appear during one of Nina's songs as Miranda

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:03.940
has said that "Spiritually, he's much closer
to the character of Nina" than Usnavi who

00:10:03.940 --> 00:10:05.920
he played on stage.

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:10.440
Miranda's wife, Vanessa Nadal, gets a cameo
too at the end in the water hydrant scene.

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:15.600
Director Jon Chu keeps the family theme going
as well, with his mum Ruth getting a secret

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:20.810
shout-out in the ghost sign for "Ruth's Bridal"
hidden beneath the sign for Daniela's Salon.

00:10:20.810 --> 00:10:25.630
And I spotted another Chu family hat-tip on
Vanessa's rental application where the real

00:10:25.630 --> 00:10:31.010
estate agency, Willow Amelia &amp; Co, is named
after Chu's daughter, whose name was inspired

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:34.960
by one of his favourite films growing up,
the 1980s fantasy movie Willow.

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:39.310
And there's even more tributes to Chu's family
in the winning lottery numbers.

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:44.000
His wife's birthday is the fifth month of
the year; Chu's daughter was born in July

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:49.350
2016, that's 7-16; and 33 is Chu's favourite
number.

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:54.350
As for number 26, that's the date his second
child was born in 2019.

00:10:54.350 --> 00:10:58.980
And Chu decided to name him after the movie,
calling him Jonathan Heights Chu.

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:04.210
A few more cameos in the film include Quiara
Alegría Hudes, who wrote the book for In

00:11:04.210 --> 00:11:08.530
The Heights, and Javier Muñoz who's played
both Usnavi and Hamilton on stage.

00:11:08.530 --> 00:11:12.730
And, according to the Hollywood Reporter,
Anthony Ramos's mother and sister also have

00:11:12.730 --> 00:11:13.891
cameos in the movie too.

00:11:13.891 --> 00:11:19.020
And to discover all the things you might have
missed in Hamilton as well as secret characters,

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:21.440
tap here to watch my full musicals playlist.

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:23.420
So, what did you think of In The Heights?

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:26.690
And did you notice any other cool details
in the movie?

00:11:26.690 --> 00:11:28.630
Let me know in the comments below.

00:11:28.630 --> 00:11:32.320
If you enjoyed this, then I really appreciate
a thumbs-up and a share.

